WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.442
Allocation defects often appear under edge conditions: error paths, partial construction, unusual input sizes, shutdown, retry logic, and concurrency.

2
00:00:07.442 --> 00:00:14.885
Tests that cover only successful creation and normal cleanup miss many of the lifecycle paths where ownership mistakes appear.

3
00:00:14.885 --> 00:00:19.193
Sanitizers can reveal memory and lifetime defects during development and CI.

4
00:00:19.193 --> 00:00:25.461
AddressSanitizer, LeakSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, debug library modes, and related tooling can make hidden defects visible while tests

5
00:00:25.461 --> 00:00:27.027
exercise the relevant code.

6
00:00:27.027 --> 00:00:30.944
They do not replace design, but they give valuable evidence.

7
00:00:30.944 --> 00:00:37.603
Tests should cover cleanup, ownership transfer, allocation failure behavior where practical, boundary sizes, repeated create-destroy cycles,

8
00:00:37.603 --> 00:00:39.170
shutdown, and partial construction.

9
00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:46.221
A good test suite verifies that resources are released and that failed construction does not expose ambiguous objects.

10
00:00:46.221 --> 00:00:51.705
Static analysis and compiler warnings can flag ownership, allocation, and cleanup patterns worth review.

11
00:00:51.705 --> 00:01:00.322
These tools are most useful when the team triages findings, documents intentional exceptions, and turns real issues into fixes rather than background

12
00:01:00.322 --> 00:01:00.714
noise.

13
00:01:00.714 --> 00:01:03.456
CI should make memory-safety checks repeatable.

14
00:01:03.456 --> 00:01:09.331
Tool findings should become tracked engineering defects with owners, fixes, and regression tests where appropriate.

15
00:01:09.331 --> 00:01:16.382
Allocation safety improves when testing and review are part of normal delivery rather than a separate cleanup effort.

16
00:01:16.382 --> 00:01:19.907
Review practices should connect tool findings back to design.

17
00:01:19.907 --> 00:01:26.174
If a leak appears in a failure path, ask which owner should have released the resource.

18
00:01:26.174 --> 00:01:34.400
If a boundary test fails, ask whether the size rule belongs in a parser, factory, allocator wrapper, or higher-level policy.
